        "text": "38"
      },
      {
        "y": 24.7,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 337.8,
        "tag": "title",
        "text": "Talking about language"
      },
      {
        "y": 70.7,
        "x0": 75.1,
        "x1": 338.3,
        "tag": "title",
        "text": "A Specialist terms for talking about language"
      },
      {
        "y": 88.4,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 256.2,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "Syntax: the grammar and word order"
      },
      {
        "y": 100.4,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 498.9,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "Different languages express modality in different ways. [meanings such as possibility and necessity]"
      },
      {
        "y": 112.4,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 355,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "English does it with modal verbs like must, could and should."
      },
      {
        "y": 124.4,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 368.6,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "Phonology: the sound system, i.e. pronunciation and intonation"
      },
      {
        "y": 136.4,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 507.9,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "All languages have phonemes, such as /b/ and /v/. [different sounds that distinguish meanings] English"
      },
      {
        "y": 148.4,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 403.8,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "has ten diphthongs. [sounds made by combining vowels, such as /eɪ/ and /aʊ/]"
      },
      {
        "y": 160.4,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 269.1,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "Lexicon: specialised term for vocabulary"
      },
      {
        "y": 172.4,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 364.6,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "Orthography: specialised term for writing systems and spelling"
      },
      {
        "y": 184.4,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 510.3,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "The English alphabet has 26 characters. [letters or symbols] Some writing systems, such as Chinese,"
      },
      {
        "y": 196.4,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 503.2,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "are not alphabetic but have pictograms or ideograms. [characters representing pictures] [characters"
      },
      {
        "y": 209.5,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 208.5,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "representing ideas/concepts]"
      },
      {
        "y": 220.4,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 251.2,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "Morphology: how words are formed"
      },
      {
        "y": 232.4,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 476.4,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "In English, there are three morphemes in unthinkable: un, think and able. [units of meaning]"
      },
      {
        "y": 253.5,
        "x0": 74.7,
        "x1": 354.8,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "B Other useful words for talking about language"
      },
      {
        "y": 271.2,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 505.1,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "Many words in English are polysemous. [they have a number of different meanings] The meaning will"
      },
      {
        "y": 283.2,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 376.1,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "usually be clear from its context. [the language or situation around it]"
      },
      {
        "y": 295.2,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 515.8,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "Words are often used in a metaphorical way – a way that is not literal, e.g. to see the light, meaning"
      },
      {
        "y": 307.4,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 351.5,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "‘to understand something’ rather than literally to see a light."
      },
      {
        "y": 319.2,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 519.4,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "Language usage inevitably changes with time. [the way words are used] Some words become obsolete"
      },
      {
        "y": 331.2,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 351.1,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "while new words are coined. [fall out of use] [of words, created]"
      },
      {
        "y": 343.2,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 504.2,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "Any language has a number of different registers. [style of language used in a particular situation, e.g."
      },
      {
        "y": 355.2,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 503.2,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "formal, informal] Jargon is used to describe a specific type of language that is used by a particular"
      },
      {
        "y": 367.2,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 436.4,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "group of people sharing a job or interest, e.g. military jargon, computer jargon."
      },
      {
        "y": 388.3,
        "x0": 75,
        "x1": 258.6,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "C Using language well or badly"
      },
      {
        "y": 406.1,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 510.5,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "Someone who uses language well can be described as articulate [able to express their ideas clearly in"
      },
      {
        "y": 418.1,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 515.1,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "words] or eloquent. [able to express ideas or opinions clearly, in a way that may influence others] They may"
      },
      {
        "y": 430.1,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 501.5,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "express something in a succinct way [clearly and using few words], and they may always be able to"
      },
      {
        "y": 442.1,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 515.8,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "think of the mot juste. [French for ‘correct word’ – exactly the right word or phrase for a particular situation]"
      },
      {
        "y": 454.1,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 507.9,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "Someone who does not use language well can be described as inarticulate. [unable to express their"
      },
      {
        "y": 466.1,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 491,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "ideas clearly in words] If you are speechless or lost for words, you are so surprised that you are"
      },
      {
        "y": 478.1,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 514.3,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "unable to say anything. If you put your foot in it, you say something without thinking carefully and"
      },
      {
        "y": 490.3,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 243.6,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "may upset or embarrass someone."
      }
    ],
